{
  "dom_id": "step-171320",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Quadratic Functions Containing an Isolated Perfect Square",
  "problem": "What is the inverse of $f(x) = (x+4)^2 -3$ for $x\\geq -4?$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny = (x+4)^2 -3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx = (y+4)^2 -3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y+4)^{2}-3 \\\\ x+3 & =(y+4)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & =y+4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}-4 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because if $x \\geq -4$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is $f^{-1} \\geq -4.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x + 3} - 4\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
